First, the property of being 'orthogonal': A set of vectors \(\{v_1, v_2, \ldots, v_n\}\) is orthogonal if for any pair of distinct vectors \(v_i\) and \(v_j\), their inner product is zero, i.e., \(\langle v_i, v_j \rangle = 0\). This ensures that each vector is perpendicular to every other vector in the set.

Second, the property of normalized length or 'unit length': Each vector in the set must have a norm (or length) of 1, i.e., \(\|v_i\| = 1\) for all vectors \(v_i\) in the system. This condition ensures that each vector is not only perpendicular to others but also is of equal and normalized size.

Together, these two conditions define an orthonormal system of vectors in the Hilbert space, ensuring both orthogonality and normalization of each vector.
</REASONING>
